00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:22.990
So this is my first speaking
engagement under the Deloitte

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:25.610
banner, so please be nice to me.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.340
I'm not used to working
for a giant firm.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:30.700
I'm still in
startup mode, so I'm

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.430
getting used to working
for a big company.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.350
So part of our
go-to-market was around

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:38.410
building enterprise-grade
technology

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:43.660
to answer the challenge of
transformation to cloud, right?

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.840
You know, I'm old
and don't be fooled.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.960
I'm very old.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.710
That's what happens
when you get acquired.

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:54.070
You can get pretty, go do a
makeover, and everything else.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:58.930
So I spent a lot of time solving
a lot of these challenges

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:00.910
for decades, right?

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:05.620
And anybody who's ever
done this, it's not easy.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:09.100
OK, and it's not
just the technology.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.860
People can make cool software
to answer problems and deal

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.270
with challenges.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:16.150
The challenge comes
with business--

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:18.310
understanding the
business reason

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:20.200
why transformation exists--

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.670
and that's what
makes it complicated

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:24.430
because I can make
software that can

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.830
move thousands of workloads.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:28.390
But if your business
can only take

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:31.990
one workload moving a month,
then that's just what happens.

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:34.390
So what I'm here
to talk about today

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:37.660
is understanding really
what the complexities are

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:40.750
in this type of business
and really understanding

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.032
how to get there
and how we do it.

00:03:43.032 --> 00:03:44.740
And that's the reason
why I'm here today.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:45.740
So let's talk about it.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:53.560
So if you look at specifically
the cost associated

00:03:53.560 --> 00:04:00.100
with transformation, $3,000
is usually the amount of it

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.010
takes to move one
server to the cloud.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:05.755
That used to be a
lot more when you

00:04:05.755 --> 00:04:08.350
were talking about a data
center enterprise transformation

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:09.620
exercise.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:12.670
So the cost has gone
down, but manually, it's

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:15.100
still an extreme challenge.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.630
How do you walk
into your clients

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:21.399
today and talk about one server
costing $3,000 from a services

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:24.550
perspective to
move to the cloud?

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.950
That's how many
months it'll take

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:31.750
after a manual process of
moving this workload into cloud.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.300
35 months to break even.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:39.520
And then, you talk
about 80% of the team

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.760
will fail to achieve what
the client is looking for.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:43.900
So what does this tell you?

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:47.290
It costs a lot of money,
it'll take you three years

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:50.590
to break even, and your
customer will be unhappy

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.490
80% of the time.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:55.370
Wow, let's go to
business together.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:57.740
That sounds like a
winning strategy, right?

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:58.690
No.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:04.210
So the reality is automation
solves challenges at scale.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:08.980
I was preaching this eight
years as the head of my startup.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:11.230
I still continue to preach it.

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:15.510
Automation allows you
to have repeatability.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:18.460
It solves for human
error, and it can actually

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:23.850
save in return a significant
return on investment.

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:25.974
So how do we do it, right?

00:05:25.974 --> 00:05:27.140
What are we concerned about?

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:28.940
What are the challenges?

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:31.460
So part of what our
platform did and still

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.370
continues to do as
part of Deloitte

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:37.250
is look at these factors
when planning and addressing

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.020
some of these challenges.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.990
So the first thing
you want to do--

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:43.700
and I always tell people this.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:48.710
My successes in my past life
and up until today, planning

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.980
is a significant part of
any successful engagement.

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:58.820
I believe 80% of your successful
engagement comes from planning.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:01.200
It doesn't matter if
you have a magic tool.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.680
Doesn't matter if you
have a genius that can

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:07.400
make servers work magically.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.780
It's planning because I can
give you a great weapon,

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.890
and if I don't show
you how to use it,

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:16.010
you might point it at yourself.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:19.700
So really, from a
strategic perspective,

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.470
the planning is critical.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:24.410
And so how do we approach this?

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.210
So the first thing
that we do, and we'll

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:28.150
start all the way to the left--

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.470
well my left, your right--

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:33.080
is comprehensive
affinity reporting.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.060
What does that mean?

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:37.670
Through the automation,
we're going to go ahead

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:41.180
and map all the
interconnectivities

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:43.160
from your application stack.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:44.870
Now, you might say--

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:48.320
and you guys are technologists,
most of the people

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.420
here are technologists--

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:52.000
well, we have monitoring tools.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.890
We have all kinds of stuff.

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:55.650
You're right.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:59.780
There's a lot of tools that do
certain jobs, but what we did

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:04.200
was create one
tool to do one job.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.880
So if you're trying to put a
screw into a wall-- and I'm

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.900
not very handy.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:14.360
In my earlier days, I used a
hammer because I'm just lazy.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:16.400
I know it's got a
little star thing in it,

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.010
but you can still hammer
that picture in, can't you?

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:21.830
The minute you hammer
that screw into a wall

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:24.860
and you hang a
picture on it, you

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:28.460
know it falls down because
that screw should not

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:29.930
have been hammered in.

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.900
But just like
anything else, we want

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:35.970
to use hammers to lay in screws.

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:37.880
So we'll get a
monitoring solution

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:42.500
and say, oh, that'll help
me do my infinity mapping.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:44.270
The reality is is
that you really

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.670
need the right tool
for the right job.

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:51.050
So in this instance, we actually
designed this technology

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.020
for this specific exercise.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:56.450
And the exercise was
trying to understand

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:58.850
how these applications
are communicating

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:03.800
for the specific
reason to move them.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.260
We're not discovering it
because we think it's important,

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:08.824
we think it's cool,
and it's great to know.

00:08:08.824 --> 00:08:10.490
If we're going to
move this application,

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:11.672
it has to be connected.

00:08:11.672 --> 00:08:13.130
And if it's connected
to something,

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:14.540
that also needs to move.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:16.700
That's how that was designed.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:21.440
Secondly, it removes
subjective reporting.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:22.400
What does that mean?

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:24.890
Everybody's been here,
worked for a company.

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:27.800
Turnover, attrition,
people leave.

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:30.290
Information leaves with people.

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:32.809
I can't trust the
third generation

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:35.600
guy who owns an
application he's just been

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:37.616
managing that he didn't build.

00:08:37.616 --> 00:08:38.990
I'm not saying I
can't trust him,

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:42.409
but he probably
doesn't trust himself.

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:44.990
I think I know how
this thing works.

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:49.310
Well, you don't want
to bet your career

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:52.250
on what somebody thinks they
may know about somebody else's

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:53.100
work.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.850
So what this does
is it eliminates

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:59.780
the process of guessing
or interviewing

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:04.790
a gentleman about a
potential application stack.

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:07.700
The need for a comprehensive
view of the current environment

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:10.490
or maintain current
state of infrastructure.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:13.610
I'm sure everyone's
used a CMDB to do work.

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:16.250
Everyone in here, CMDB?

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:19.280
I go to clients all the
time, and I say, OK,

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:21.590
let me see your CMDB.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:23.810
They usually give me
the CMDB like this.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:28.750
I sent you the CMDB.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:31.990
OK, that's how they do it.

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:34.530
That's not very
confidence building

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:38.160
when I have to plan a
transformation on a banking

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:41.840
client that can't
suffer any downtime.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:44.010
That's their CMDB.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:46.840
So I really don't
want to trust it.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:49.980
So what I do is basically
through the collection period,

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:55.800
I create a up-to-date inventory
of the client's infrastructure.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:57.150
That's significant.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.440
It might sound like it's easy.

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:01.250
I'll give you a
real case scenario.

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:04.890
Went to a client, they
had 10,000 servers.

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:07.980
We discovered 10,000
servers, just not

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:10.479
the 10,000 they told us about.

00:10:10.479 --> 00:10:12.270
That was a really funny,
interesting story.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:16.440
I sat down and said, customer,
here's 7,000 of your servers.

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:17.955
We can't find the 3,000.

00:10:17.955 --> 00:10:21.120
The client's like,
we have 10,000.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.550
I said, well, we found seven.

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:27.340
And they said, well,
what about these?

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:28.730
They did the same thing.

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:30.850
Here's 3,000 more servers.

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:32.280
Can you look at those?

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.170
Next thing you know, we
reconcile their environment,

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:37.350
the client was happy,
now they have a plan.

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:41.330
But imagine if the
same client would

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:44.970
have went off their CMDB to
do any type of transformation

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:45.960
activity?

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:48.790
It would have been
complete disaster.

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:51.660
So the mapping portion
and planning portion

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:54.030
is significant.

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:58.920
Outside of clouds, servers
are underused by 43%.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:02.880
We all know what
VMware used to be.

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:05.280
Everybody here who ever
been a VMware person?

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.620
I was certified in
VMware in my past.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:11.230
What do we do?

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:12.840
We have templates.

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:15.360
We got template a,
b, c, d, e, f, g.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:17.860
We would coordinate
and build environments

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:19.770
in a templatized way.

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:23.010
Well, that means we'd have these
giant servers that we're not

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:25.490
really using and maximizing.

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:29.240
Do we want to bring that
same platform to cloud?

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:30.990
And the reason why I'm
talking about this,

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:35.220
this is not a exercise
in manually building

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:36.170
infrastructure.

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:37.470
You don't want to do that.

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:38.970
We want to use the
Cloud Automation.

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:40.030
We want to use scripting.

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:43.050
We want to use our technology to
build out this infrastructure.

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:47.340
We do not want to repeat our
failure on-premise in cloud.

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:50.550
So by mapping it,
we're going to tell you

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:54.900
how your current servers
are being currently utilized

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:57.180
so that you can
size your servers

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:59.215
appropriately for future state.

00:11:59.215 --> 00:12:00.840
That's important
because, first of all,

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:02.760
it cuts down costs immediately.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:04.060
I'll give an example.

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:07.620
Anybody here who uses Microsoft
SQL licensing, for instance.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:11.340
Your licensing is attached
to the size of your servers

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:12.720
that you're moving.

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:15.750
If I move a server
that I'm using 43% of

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:19.320
and I have multiple cores
and multiple disks and CPU,

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:22.249
my Microsoft licensing
costs are this high.

00:12:22.249 --> 00:12:23.790
You can get in front
of your customer

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:28.350
and change not only the
way their computer is being

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:32.250
consumed or how much
of it, but save cost

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:35.130
to the customer with
their cloud adoption move.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:38.070
So you can take that
same conversation

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:40.860
and take it from a
technology perspective, which

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:42.810
is the conversations
I used to have,

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:45.900
and move it into a business
perspective and conversations

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:49.020
around taking money back
into your own business.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:52.170
That differentiation.

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:55.340
So let's look at what we
did and what we didn't.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:58.950
So now we've moved
on from there.

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:02.670
Here's the other thing I
want to make a point about.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:06.510
When we do the mapping, we
also right size the instance

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:08.880
because we talk
about utilization.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:14.520
We also monitor performance
metrics, CPU, RAM, disks,

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:18.600
to determine if they
need a specialized AMI.

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:21.210
Should they use a
disk intensive AMI?

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:25.680
Should they use an AMI that
is for CPU intensitivity?

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:30.210
Whatever that we prescribe
the customer's getting

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:33.390
ready the benefits of cloud
almost immediately after they

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:34.920
get there.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:36.950
So we're cutting costs,
we're cutting down

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:40.200
their compute
footprint, and then

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:43.110
we're also saving them
money from licensing.

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:47.130
But we're also saving them money
off the size of the instances

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:49.200
they're provisioning.

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:50.920
So it's a significant
differentiation.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:53.100
So let's talk about migrating.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.890
So one of the things that we
used to do-- and this was ATA.

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:58.440
It means any-to-any.

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:01.590
We're here at Google, so
we're any-to-Google today.

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:06.480
But part of the platform
removes the dependency

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.470
on infrastructure.

00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:12.360
So if the client is on AWS,
they can come to Google,

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:15.420
from VMware to
Google, Google back.

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:16.450
Doesn't really matter.

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:20.306
We can support portability
between any of the structures,

00:14:20.306 --> 00:14:21.430
so that's a big difference.

00:14:21.430 --> 00:14:23.460
So there's no vendor lock.

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:27.270
So you're really going to get
the best value for cloud not

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:30.990
just from a cost perspective
but a performance perspective.

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:34.920
So I don't want to put you
on the Walmart of clouds.

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:36.920
We don't want to go
in that direction.

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:41.785
What we want to do is
balance capability and cost.

00:14:41.785 --> 00:14:43.410
Those are the kind
of conversations you

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:44.842
can have with your customers.

00:14:44.842 --> 00:14:46.300
Now, I've been at
Google for years.

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:49.170
I know what their
cloud is capable of,

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:51.180
but that doesn't mean
they're the market leader

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:53.920
from a consumption perspective.

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:55.560
So there's some
conversations that we

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:59.850
could have about how the
client can save money and take

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:02.280
advantage of cloud capability.

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:08.566
Now, manually migrating a server
is 10 times more intensive.

00:15:08.566 --> 00:15:10.440
I've been there, I've
done manual migrations.

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:11.856
Those are the last
things you want

00:15:11.856 --> 00:15:17.057
to do because of the complexity,
the chance that it could fail.

00:15:17.057 --> 00:15:18.640
Those are things you
don't want to do.

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:21.780
So with automation,
it also cuts down cost

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:24.000
and reduces the effort.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:25.110
I'll give an example.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:27.570
Last month-- well, it was
about two months ago--

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:31.410
I was in India, and we
were doing a launch.

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:36.330
And we migrated 1,000 servers,
and I think the whole process--

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:38.130
you know, they
weren't big servers--

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:41.190
but it was like 18
minutes to launch.

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:43.590
And they all finished
within seven to eight hours.

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:44.250
A thousand.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:45.690
That's a lot of servers.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:47.550
No enterprise is
going to do that,

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:49.530
but we did it in our
environment just to see

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:51.120
what our capabilities were.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:54.510
But just imagine
scale, what you can

00:15:54.510 --> 00:16:00.150
solve when you remove barriers
such as human rest, sleep,

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:02.970
and food.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.060
So there would be no eating
on data center floors

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:06.268
if you got automation, right?

00:16:06.268 --> 00:16:07.120
So there you go.

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:14.510
OK, this is going backwards.

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:16.950
I just want to make sure I
haven't been talking this long.

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:20.240
All right, sometimes I
forget how long I talk.

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:23.080
So as far as management, so
one of the other things that we

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:26.530
talk about with cloud is every
time you have a cloud platform,

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:27.880
there's a cloud console.

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:29.170
There's a management portal.

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:30.820
There's a skills transfer.

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:32.452
There's guys who need to know--

00:16:32.452 --> 00:16:33.910
if you have a
multi-cloud approach,

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:34.930
you have Google experts.

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:36.400
You have VM guys on-premise.

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:38.290
You may have another
cloud provider.

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:42.340
So when you have the
capability of management,

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:44.770
you're able to manage
across all platforms,

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:46.390
have one single pane of glass.

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:48.400
So with the product
and the capabilities

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:54.250
that we have at Deloitte, we're
able to provision Greenfield,

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:58.120
Brownfield, portable
apps, move applications.

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:00.280
We're building out in
our roadmap microservices

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:02.410
conversions from VMware.

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:04.750
So we can automatically
take, say, virtual machines,

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:07.540
convert them into market
services in different types

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:09.931
of containerization platforms.

00:17:09.931 --> 00:17:11.680
So those are some of
the things that we're

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:14.650
thinking about for the future.

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:17.710
And then, of course, we have
our own mitigate platform,

00:17:17.710 --> 00:17:19.900
which is a DR platform,
which is currently

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:22.329
running on a competitive
cloud, but we are bringing

00:17:22.329 --> 00:17:25.280
that to Google in the future.

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:28.052
So what are Deloitte's
cloud capabilities

00:17:28.052 --> 00:17:29.260
from a migration perspective?

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:31.450
We talked about
the journey, right?

00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:34.580
The client already normally has
decided what they want to do.

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:37.460
They may not know
where they want to go.

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:39.580
So those are the kind
of conversations we want

00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:42.220
to have with our customers.

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:44.140
Build what's best.

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:48.516
I'm a proponent on
movement and transition,

00:17:48.516 --> 00:17:50.890
but that doesn't mean that
lift and shift is the only way

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:51.460
to get there.

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:53.950
Obviously, there's
multiple different ways.

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:55.780
You can re-platform.

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:57.220
We can rebuild applications.

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:58.930
You can optimize in cloud.

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:02.350
All of those outcomes
are possible when

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:06.490
you know multi-tiered approach.

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:08.350
And then advancement
in operations.

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:11.160
One of the biggest issues
I've seen in cloud--

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:14.220
is not cloud, is
not technology--

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:16.540
is people.

00:18:16.540 --> 00:18:19.480
Where are the people to run this
and manage this environment?

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:22.360
I assume some of you guys
are in the audience today.

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:25.840
But the reality is
the biggest obstacle

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:29.810
I've seen to change
is the customer team.

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:32.140
We have the leadership
team that wants cloud,

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:37.655
we have the business that wants
cloud, but cloud is a new job.

00:18:37.655 --> 00:18:38.530
Where are the skills?

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:40.280
Where are the people
that can manage this?

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:43.180
How do we transition
that conversation

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:46.120
to our team to educate them
on how to support, manage,

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:48.340
and understand the
DevOps kind of cloud

00:18:48.340 --> 00:18:52.310
approach versus a typical
infrastructure approach?

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:55.735
And that's from a perspective
of advancing operations.

00:18:55.735 --> 00:18:57.970
So when we talk about
initiating, engaging,

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:00.750
converge, mobilize
in an adoption scale,

00:19:00.750 --> 00:19:03.310
that's all cloud and cloud does.

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:05.060
But getting there
is the challenge,

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:09.410
and that's what we're talking
about with our product set.

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:14.140
So what is a
migration lifecycle?

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:16.990
This is our product set
as it was designed prior

00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:19.630
to acquisition, and
this is actually

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:24.340
how it lives inside our
Deloitte services team now.

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:28.150
Phase one, as we talked about,
is our application analysis

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:29.710
migration prioritization.

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:31.500
Migration prioritization
is significant.

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:34.540
I'm going to talk a little
bit about that today.

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:36.970
Migration prioritization
is what happens

00:19:36.970 --> 00:19:38.890
when you plan a migration.

00:19:38.890 --> 00:19:41.650
That means you may have
thousands of workloads.

00:19:41.650 --> 00:19:43.870
You're not going to move
your SAP workloads on day

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:46.300
one of your migration plan.

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:48.040
That's not happening.

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:50.650
I know you guys want to do
that, but no, you can't do it.

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:53.530
So prioritization
will allow you to--

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:55.060
and this is through automation.

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:59.560
We will tell you which
workloads are easy to go first.

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:02.690
That's significant because
it's going to enable your team

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:04.780
to know, have the answer.

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:10.357
OK, guys, we're moving our
enterprise in 50 waves.

00:20:10.357 --> 00:20:12.190
These are the waves,
and this is how they're

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:13.480
going to be broken down.

00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:16.420
We'll actually give you
a plan on how to execute

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:20.050
your transformation safely.

00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:22.480
Automated infrastructure
analysis, we talked about that.

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:23.530
Performance metrics.

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:26.740
We talked about
sizing, rightsizing.

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:31.440
We talked about what cloud is
best suited for your workload.

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:34.810
We'll even tell
you a cost analysis

00:20:34.810 --> 00:20:39.575
on how each workload
group or cluster is best

00:20:39.575 --> 00:20:41.770
suited price-wise
for which cloud

00:20:41.770 --> 00:20:45.470
because, at the end of the day
when the rubber hits the road,

00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:48.575
you have to have a conversation
of cost with your customers.

00:20:48.575 --> 00:20:50.950
You have to understand that's
going to be a conversation.

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:54.640
Well, we're going to be able
to provide that for you.

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:57.370
Readiness planning, so we
talked about this a little bit

00:20:57.370 --> 00:20:58.780
earlier as well.

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:00.520
Migration task
force setup, we're

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:02.710
going to put a team together.

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:04.570
Wave planning, we
talked about that.

00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:06.000
Security and governance.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:10.170
When you get to the cloud, you
still have security concerns.

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:12.580
You still have maybe even
more security concerns

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:14.050
than you had on-premise.

00:21:14.050 --> 00:21:17.440
All of that is important
and part of your strategy

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:19.450
of planning.

00:21:19.450 --> 00:21:23.320
So before you move, you have
to have the house set up.

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:25.320
You have to have the
foundation set up.

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:30.340
I always talk about migration
as, essentially, the movers,

00:21:30.340 --> 00:21:33.425
but you don't consult the
movers until you have a house.

00:21:33.425 --> 00:21:35.800
You have a foundation, you
have walls, you have plumbing,

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:37.060
you have electricity.

00:21:37.060 --> 00:21:39.132
Once that's all set up,
that's your security,

00:21:39.132 --> 00:21:40.090
that's your governance.

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:42.280
You have to have doors in
the front of your house

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:43.840
before you move
your furniture in.

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:46.690
So all of that is significant.

00:21:46.690 --> 00:21:48.380
Foundational services planning.

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:51.160
What are you planning to do
when you get to the cloud?

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:52.070
What is your plan?

00:21:52.070 --> 00:21:53.470
What is your strategy?

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:54.704
Is it just a lift and shift?

00:21:54.704 --> 00:21:55.870
Infrastructure as a service?

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:57.130
Are you using PAS?

00:21:57.130 --> 00:21:58.870
Are you using
database as a service?

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:01.090
All of those things,
all those conversations

00:22:01.090 --> 00:22:03.290
need to have been had
and everything needed

00:22:03.290 --> 00:22:05.070
have been set up.

00:22:05.070 --> 00:22:07.640
Pilot landing zone build-out.

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:10.630
It is as describe, pilot.

00:22:10.630 --> 00:22:13.990
You've got to prove this
concept actually is viable

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:18.760
before you make a wholehearted
decision to move to the cloud.

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:19.930
And then migration.

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:23.500
For instance, we talk about
the waves, the execution.

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:26.410
All of that is part of
our readiness planning.

00:22:26.410 --> 00:22:29.527
Then we talk about execution.

00:22:29.527 --> 00:22:31.360
And all of the products
I talked about here,

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:33.280
ATAVision, ATAMotion
handle that.

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:37.030
This is really the
next level, the future,

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:38.620
of what we feel
where cloud is going

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:41.590
to go from a
transformation perspective.

00:22:41.590 --> 00:22:45.310
Part of our phase three
is OS transformation.

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:47.260
Part of the capabilities
in the platform

00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:51.550
allow you to transform
workloads to different types.

00:22:51.550 --> 00:22:56.230
For instance, you can
take a Red Hat on-premise

00:22:56.230 --> 00:23:03.460
and convert it to
CentOS on Google.

00:23:03.460 --> 00:23:09.780
You can take AWS Linux and
convert it into CentOS or Red

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:11.390
Hat running on Google.

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:13.830
That's significant
because see, AWS

00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:16.829
thought they won the game when
they created Amazon Linux.

00:23:16.829 --> 00:23:19.120
Where we cracked the code,
and we can take Amazon Linux

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:22.810
and convert it into
Cent and Red Hat.

00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:26.050
That's huge because now, the
monopoly and the vendor lock

00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:28.620
is a complete broke.

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:29.870
Now, I'll give you an example.

00:23:29.870 --> 00:23:32.320
We had a customer that
was a retail customer that

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:34.570
wasn't happy with
their relationship

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:37.990
with that other provider
I just mentioned.

00:23:37.990 --> 00:23:41.240
They needed to move to Google,
but they were running AWS Linux

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:43.310
Paravirtual.

00:23:43.310 --> 00:23:45.810
Anybody know what
Paravirtual VMs are?

00:23:45.810 --> 00:23:47.620
Oh, it's really ugly.

00:23:47.620 --> 00:23:50.680
Google doesn't
support Paravirtual.

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:56.230
We converted them to
HVM on Google on CentOS.

00:23:56.230 --> 00:23:57.880
So they took the
whole enterprise,

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:01.570
migrated it off,
put it on Google,

00:24:01.570 --> 00:24:04.600
converted it from
Paravirtual to HVM VMs,

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:08.550
and turned them into
CentOS on Google.

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:10.160
It's like magic, really.

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:12.890
I didn't do it personally, but
I had a really smart team that

00:24:12.890 --> 00:24:15.710
came up with this capability.

00:24:15.710 --> 00:24:18.050
So what else are we
doing with transform?

00:24:18.050 --> 00:24:20.900
We're taking on-premise
databases, like Oracle,

00:24:20.900 --> 00:24:22.160
and converting them.

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:24.415
So Postgres and
MySQL on database

00:24:24.415 --> 00:24:26.310
as a service platforms.

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:29.190
That's the next level
of transformation.

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:31.850
So when we talk
about lift and shift,

00:24:31.850 --> 00:24:33.890
we're changing the vernacular.

00:24:33.890 --> 00:24:36.230
We're not just going to
take your workload as is.

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:40.160
We're doing conversions
of technology now.

00:24:40.160 --> 00:24:44.510
So if anybody snuck in here
from any competitive product,

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:48.290
I'm sorry you had to
hear that because that's

00:24:48.290 --> 00:24:50.160
at least three years
for work for you guys.

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:50.840
You better get started.

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:52.964
I think you should leave
the session now, actually.

00:24:52.964 --> 00:24:54.690
Get started immediately.

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:56.840
But that's where we're headed.

00:24:56.840 --> 00:24:59.990
We're pushing the
envelope of stack.

00:24:59.990 --> 00:25:03.210
So all of the lift and
shift is still happening,

00:25:03.210 --> 00:25:04.700
but we're going to
change the game.

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:08.830
We're talking about taking
VMs, as I mentioned earlier,

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:14.940
containerizing them through
automation, not manual effort.

00:25:14.940 --> 00:25:16.970
So we already have guys
working on this today,

00:25:16.970 --> 00:25:21.080
doing live projects today where
they're going in with clients

00:25:21.080 --> 00:25:24.370
and containerizing their
network, their infrastructure,

00:25:24.370 --> 00:25:26.360
through automation.

00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:27.932
Huge.

00:25:27.932 --> 00:25:29.640
So this is where all
the fun's happening.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:34.285
So when you ask me about the
transition to acquisition,

00:25:34.285 --> 00:25:35.660
this is the fun
I'm going to have

00:25:35.660 --> 00:25:37.700
for the next couple
of years here

00:25:37.700 --> 00:25:40.550
at Deloitte is
moving the envelope

00:25:40.550 --> 00:25:43.550
and challenging all the
other providers in our space

00:25:43.550 --> 00:25:46.170
to meet that challenge.

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:49.400
And then finally,
Mirror cutover.

00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:53.790
So enterprise workloads
are extremely complex.

00:25:53.790 --> 00:25:54.960
We all know this.

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:57.170
SAP, extremely complex,
highly transactional.

00:25:57.170 --> 00:25:58.790
How do we move them?

00:25:58.790 --> 00:26:01.130
We're listed as one
of the only providers

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:05.420
globally that is verified
by multiple cloud providers

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:08.900
as a tool of technology of
choice for SAP workload.

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:10.280
And the reason why is this--

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:11.270
Mirror.

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:15.470
So part of our engine
constantly mirrors delta changes

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:20.000
to the workloads as
they're running live.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:24.140
So we migrate all these
complex workloads live

00:26:24.140 --> 00:26:26.150
and we're constantly
synchronizing them.

00:26:26.150 --> 00:26:29.660
So when you're ready to
flip the switch, voila.

00:26:29.660 --> 00:26:32.060
You're on the other
side of the fence.

00:26:32.060 --> 00:26:35.060
It didn't take you a
three-hour cutover window,

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:37.790
and your enterprise
workloads are intact.

00:26:37.790 --> 00:26:40.520
We moved over
2,000 SAP workloads

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:43.470
in the last 18 months.

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:47.360
So when people talk about
how hard SAP is to move,

00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:48.200
we just do it.

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:49.610
It's like everyday work for us.

00:26:49.610 --> 00:26:50.870
So we don't have a
problem because that's how

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:52.119
the architecture was designed.

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:58.000
So this looks like an
infomercial for my company,

00:26:58.000 --> 00:26:59.050
but it's really not.

00:26:59.050 --> 00:27:01.230
It's really Deloitte's,
just so you know.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:03.560
And I keep seeing
this all over again,

00:27:03.560 --> 00:27:08.140
but I've actually walked through
many of this stuff already.

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:12.370
But we talk about
projections, migrate, manage,

00:27:12.370 --> 00:27:15.430
and then, of course, we have our
own disaster recovery product

00:27:15.430 --> 00:27:17.890
called ATAGuard, which is
running on a competitive cloud

00:27:17.890 --> 00:27:20.520
today, which will be
moved over to Google.

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:27.410
So all of these modules,
we talked about--

00:27:27.410 --> 00:27:31.820
Discovery, Motion, Transform,
Sphere, and Guard--

00:27:31.820 --> 00:27:34.040
they all fit under one platform.

00:27:34.040 --> 00:27:36.470
It's called ATASphere.

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:39.740
So ATASphere is
the central brain

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:45.470
of the platform that
allows you to control

00:27:45.470 --> 00:27:46.880
all of these manually.

00:27:46.880 --> 00:27:48.770
He had to leave because
he had three hours--

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:50.447
he has three years
to catch up on.

00:27:50.447 --> 00:27:51.530
I think I saw [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:51.530 --> 00:27:53.510
He immediately took
a picture and left.

00:27:53.510 --> 00:27:55.470
No, I'm just joking.

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:57.219
You better hurry
up, I'm just saying.

00:27:57.219 --> 00:27:58.260
I told you guys to start.

00:27:58.260 --> 00:28:00.290
Anybody else needs to
leave and start developing,

00:28:00.290 --> 00:28:01.706
you better hurry
up and leave now.

00:28:01.706 --> 00:28:05.210
You're gonna fall behind.

00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:07.220
But anyway, so the module.

00:28:07.220 --> 00:28:11.540
The platform sits on top
of all of these modules

00:28:11.540 --> 00:28:15.020
and allows you complete
control over every one.

00:28:15.020 --> 00:28:18.330
So when we talk about
a single pane of glass,

00:28:18.330 --> 00:28:22.250
imagine one pane of glass and
you can see all your compute,

00:28:22.250 --> 00:28:26.840
and have the ability to
move it, manage it, create,

00:28:26.840 --> 00:28:30.500
or protect your entire compute.

00:28:30.500 --> 00:28:33.380
I used to be a big
VMware fan, and that's

00:28:33.380 --> 00:28:36.400
why I came up with
ATASphere because if vSphere

00:28:36.400 --> 00:28:42.297
could have done this, they
would have been great.

00:28:42.297 --> 00:28:43.880
Now they're on
everybody else's cloud.

00:28:43.880 --> 00:28:44.880
What does that tell you?

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:45.830
See about decisions?

00:28:45.830 --> 00:28:48.590
How you make your decisions
will depend on how you end up.

00:28:48.590 --> 00:28:51.140
But this would have been a
significant advantage for them.

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:52.790
So that was my vision.

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:55.180
One single pane
of glass, control

00:28:55.180 --> 00:28:58.580
of your entire infrastructure,
complete visibility,

00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:00.370
complete control management.

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:02.990
That's power.

00:29:02.990 --> 00:29:05.630
So you can create
your virtual subnets.

00:29:05.630 --> 00:29:09.140
You can create virtual private
clouds in other environments.

00:29:09.140 --> 00:29:10.880
You can move your
compute anywhere

00:29:10.880 --> 00:29:14.091
you want without
bringing it down.

00:29:14.091 --> 00:29:14.590
Future.

00:29:17.390 --> 00:29:20.320
So what are we collecting?

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:24.310
This allows you to see
the entire environment,

00:29:24.310 --> 00:29:26.830
but this is some of the
reporting we give you.

00:29:26.830 --> 00:29:28.350
This is visualization.

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:30.110
These are outputs.

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:32.170
These are the cost comparisons.

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:34.930
This is all the host
data that's collected.

00:29:34.930 --> 00:29:37.730
So it's a significant
amount of granularity.

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:41.240
So when I tell people,
when you look at this,

00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:43.840
it's not just for executives.

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:48.230
A lot of these views are
not just executive driven.

00:29:48.230 --> 00:29:50.110
There's not a lot of
pictures sometimes.

00:29:50.110 --> 00:29:52.330
But when you are in
the trenches and you

00:29:52.330 --> 00:29:55.562
are trying to make critical
decisions, you need facts

00:29:55.562 --> 00:29:56.395
and you need detail.

00:29:59.530 --> 00:30:03.790
I've seen other technologies
or other folks use spreadsheets

00:30:03.790 --> 00:30:06.220
a lot manually.

00:30:06.220 --> 00:30:10.660
Before this-- remember, I've
been in this a long time--

00:30:10.660 --> 00:30:13.030
we used to have
spreadsheets, and we

00:30:13.030 --> 00:30:16.135
used to talk to customers
and interview them.

00:30:16.135 --> 00:30:19.570
And we used to
write all this down.

00:30:19.570 --> 00:30:21.330
That was planning.

00:30:21.330 --> 00:30:23.710
Think about how wrought
with error that is.

00:30:23.710 --> 00:30:25.210
I can't even make
out, is that an L?

00:30:25.210 --> 00:30:25.810
Is that a one?

00:30:25.810 --> 00:30:27.340
Is that a O?

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:28.240
Is that a zero?

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:30.010
I don't know, right?

00:30:30.010 --> 00:30:32.360
All of this stuff is
through automation.

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:35.790
So the technology is not going
to fudge that the letter.

00:30:35.790 --> 00:30:38.310
It's not going to tell you
something that's incorrect.

00:30:38.310 --> 00:30:42.220
That's why you can lay
your hat down on automation

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:46.540
many times is because
once you figure it out,

00:30:46.540 --> 00:30:50.990
it becomes a repeatable
process you can execute.

00:30:50.990 --> 00:30:53.440
So we went through this.

00:30:53.440 --> 00:30:56.080
This is just more granularity
I'm giving you guys.

00:30:56.080 --> 00:31:01.150
So 20 hours between
manual migrations, right?

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:02.800
We're talking about one by one.

00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:06.730
Maybe you build a server,
you provision a server,

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:10.750
you install the application,
you move the data,

00:31:10.750 --> 00:31:12.820
you watch it move.

00:31:12.820 --> 00:31:17.060
It gets there, you validate
it, you turn up the app.

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:18.410
OK, we're done.

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:20.080
OK, next server.

00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:24.760
Well, when you can do bunches
at a time or even dozens

00:31:24.760 --> 00:31:28.840
at a time, it takes that
effort to three hours,

00:31:28.840 --> 00:31:30.250
and then you just repeat it.

00:31:30.250 --> 00:31:33.340
So the same 10 servers--

00:31:33.340 --> 00:31:34.570
say I migrate 30 servers.

00:31:34.570 --> 00:31:36.430
It took three hours.

00:31:36.430 --> 00:31:39.500
That's 90 servers
in three hours.

00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:41.170
Even though you break
it down by hours,

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:43.840
it's the same number of
hours it took to do manually.

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:46.480
You're doing 30 at a time.

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:49.570
That's just simple
mathematics as far

00:31:49.570 --> 00:31:52.180
as efficiency is concerned.

00:31:52.180 --> 00:31:56.290
Also, secure
deployment versatility.

00:31:56.290 --> 00:31:58.240
All the transfer is encrypted.

00:31:58.240 --> 00:32:02.870
We use 256-bit encryption
inside your environment.

00:32:02.870 --> 00:32:06.150
So you already have a firewall,
you have protective networking

00:32:06.150 --> 00:32:08.380
somewhat in there I'm
assuming, and then

00:32:08.380 --> 00:32:10.160
we encrypt the data transfer.

00:32:10.160 --> 00:32:12.930
So we just add layer
and layer of security

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:18.430
in these types of scenarios
And then we talked about this.

00:32:20.990 --> 00:32:22.640
This is important.

00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:24.770
I tell people all
the time, anybody

00:32:24.770 --> 00:32:26.390
can migrate a web server.

00:32:26.390 --> 00:32:28.010
That doesn't impress me.

00:32:28.010 --> 00:32:29.120
I see it all the time.

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:33.020
I see demos, real cute
demos, small products

00:32:33.020 --> 00:32:36.590
that say, look, we migrate
a server in 15 minutes.

00:32:36.590 --> 00:32:38.770
I'm like, wow, that's
pretty impressive.

00:32:38.770 --> 00:32:40.520
What was it running?

00:32:40.520 --> 00:32:42.920
Bugzilla.

00:32:42.920 --> 00:32:44.300
Y'all can all laugh at that.

00:32:44.300 --> 00:32:45.230
That's OK.

00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:48.140
Bugzilla, that's a
laughable comment.

00:32:48.140 --> 00:32:51.740
But the reality is,
somebody tells you

00:32:51.740 --> 00:32:54.890
they're migrating Oracle
rack in 15 minutes, that's

00:32:54.890 --> 00:32:59.850
when you take them out back for
lying to you and thrash them.

00:32:59.850 --> 00:33:02.220
You can thrash them,
you know, just saying.

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:03.990
But that's the reality.

00:33:03.990 --> 00:33:08.720
The reality is the
enterprise is the challenge.

00:33:08.720 --> 00:33:11.027
Bugzilla, not so
much a challenge.

00:33:13.710 --> 00:33:16.430
So how easy is this to do?

00:33:16.430 --> 00:33:20.600
Well, I designed this product
a long time ago for anyone

00:33:20.600 --> 00:33:23.060
to use it because
my challenge was--

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:25.640
and just to give you
a little background--

00:33:25.640 --> 00:33:29.330
I was working in development.

00:33:29.330 --> 00:33:31.160
I was a storage architect.

00:33:31.160 --> 00:33:33.230
I was a networking architect.

00:33:33.230 --> 00:33:34.910
I was a Windows architect.

00:33:34.910 --> 00:33:36.260
I was a Linux architect.

00:33:36.260 --> 00:33:37.370
I was an AIX architect.

00:33:37.370 --> 00:33:38.411
And I don't mean to brag.

00:33:38.411 --> 00:33:39.920
That means I didn't
have any life.

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:42.490
That's not really something
you should be proud of.

00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:45.710
But what it helped me realize is
that every time I got in a room

00:33:45.710 --> 00:33:47.870
with all of these
people, everybody

00:33:47.870 --> 00:33:50.390
thought theirs was more
important than the next guy.

00:33:50.390 --> 00:33:52.430
And that's the reason
why I learned it

00:33:52.430 --> 00:33:54.140
because I was into
Windows, everybody

00:33:54.140 --> 00:33:55.650
disrespects the Windows guy.

00:33:55.650 --> 00:33:57.399
Everybody knows, oh,
it's the Windows guy.

00:33:57.399 --> 00:33:59.030
He needs pictures.

00:33:59.030 --> 00:33:59.990
Then, the Linux guy.

00:33:59.990 --> 00:34:02.406
Oh, we don't like him because
he really doesn't know Unix.

00:34:02.406 --> 00:34:03.800
He had to learn Linux.

00:34:03.800 --> 00:34:05.420
That's not real Unix.

00:34:05.420 --> 00:34:07.580
So then I knew the Unix guy.

00:34:07.580 --> 00:34:11.360
And the Unix guy was like, oh,
no, I meant the storage guy,

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:12.150
that guy.

00:34:12.150 --> 00:34:13.940
He had all the power
because everything

00:34:13.940 --> 00:34:16.820
was running on
high-performance storage,

00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:20.330
and who cares what the
operating system was?

00:34:20.330 --> 00:34:23.900
So I learned all of their
towers so I could sit in a room

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:26.150
and tell him to
shut up, and this

00:34:26.150 --> 00:34:28.440
is how we're going to do it.

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:31.250
I'm going to eliminate
all your jobs.

00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:33.170
I'm going to create an
interface so simple,

00:34:33.170 --> 00:34:37.460
a Windows guy can do
a migration for Unix.

00:34:37.460 --> 00:34:40.610
Oh, they didn't like that,
but when they saw it,

00:34:40.610 --> 00:34:45.060
they couldn't say anything about
it because actually worked.

00:34:45.060 --> 00:34:47.239
So when you talk about
what made me do it,

00:34:47.239 --> 00:34:50.980
I got tired of the egos, and
I got tired of the arrogance.

00:34:50.980 --> 00:34:54.469
There's a lot of
that in our field.

00:34:54.469 --> 00:34:57.380
And you guys have been in
rooms with really smart people.

00:34:57.380 --> 00:34:59.150
They didn't have
to be jerks too.

00:34:59.150 --> 00:35:00.890
They were smart, they earned it.

00:35:00.890 --> 00:35:04.190
But I got tired of that, so
I created this platform so I

00:35:04.190 --> 00:35:06.800
could say, look how easy
it is for this Windows

00:35:06.800 --> 00:35:11.050
guy on the help desk to
migrate an AIX [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:11.050 --> 00:35:12.020
to the cloud.

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:13.970
And we actually did that.

00:35:13.970 --> 00:35:15.150
So what did we do?

00:35:15.150 --> 00:35:17.690
We made things simple.

00:35:17.690 --> 00:35:21.290
We created one profile,
one visibility, one view.

00:35:21.290 --> 00:35:25.410
We created all the API hooks
to 20 different clouds and nine

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:27.620
hypervisors.

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:29.970
20 different clouds,
nine hypervisors.

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:33.740
I was talking about clouds you
probably don't even care about.

00:35:33.740 --> 00:35:36.780
CenturyLink, anybody
care about CenturyLink?

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:37.350
Nobody.

00:35:37.350 --> 00:35:41.177
Poor CenturyLink, but they
used to be a big cloud.

00:35:41.177 --> 00:35:43.010
They still are, they
still have a footprint.

00:35:43.010 --> 00:35:44.380
But that's the reality.

00:35:44.380 --> 00:35:46.670
Reality is we can go anywhere.

00:35:46.670 --> 00:35:49.250
Anywhere their workload
is, that's where we are.

00:35:49.250 --> 00:35:50.955
SoftLayer, Alibaba.

00:35:50.955 --> 00:35:53.330
I mean, I don't want to drop
too many names at the Google

00:35:53.330 --> 00:35:56.062
conference, but we know
there are other clouds.

00:35:56.062 --> 00:35:57.270
Let's talk about hypervisors.

00:35:57.270 --> 00:36:00.360
KVM, Citrix.

00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:03.380
Let's talk about Hyper-V.
Let's talk about VMware.

00:36:03.380 --> 00:36:06.470
VMware had a big foothold on
this business a long time ago.

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:08.240
It's slipping, right?

00:36:08.240 --> 00:36:10.430
So how about an
interface that lets

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:12.170
you migrate between
all those platforms

00:36:12.170 --> 00:36:15.279
through API so easy the guy
at the help desk could do it?

00:36:15.279 --> 00:36:16.070
That's what we did.

00:36:18.980 --> 00:36:23.460
So case study, what do we do?

00:36:23.460 --> 00:36:25.730
We talked about the
big number, right?

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:31.400
The big number is easy when
you don't have limitations.

00:36:31.400 --> 00:36:35.210
The way our product was designed
and built was peer-to-peer.

00:36:35.210 --> 00:36:38.870
That means the workload
process transformation exists

00:36:38.870 --> 00:36:41.160
between source and target.

00:36:41.160 --> 00:36:44.590
So as long as the
pipe is big enough,

00:36:44.590 --> 00:36:48.420
there is no processing power
being utilized by our product.

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:51.890
It's all distributed
in the architecture.

00:36:51.890 --> 00:36:57.230
That means if I have 1,000
workloads, there's 1,000 pairs.

00:36:57.230 --> 00:36:59.120
They're talking
amongst themselves.

00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:01.490
They're transferring the
data amongst themselves.

00:37:01.490 --> 00:37:03.260
They set up a client
server architecture

00:37:03.260 --> 00:37:05.420
amongst themselves.

00:37:05.420 --> 00:37:10.500
That's how you scale because you
don't care about dependencies

00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:12.210
of infrastructure.

00:37:12.210 --> 00:37:16.250
There's no worry
about, hey, do I

00:37:16.250 --> 00:37:19.640
have to create a library
to store my images

00:37:19.640 --> 00:37:21.314
and then deploy?

00:37:21.314 --> 00:37:22.980
So any of our competitors
that are still

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:26.510
here who haven't left
to start developing--

00:37:26.510 --> 00:37:27.980
I think we lost
three or four guys.

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:30.090
They had to go start.

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:33.650
So to the rest of you guys
who are going to fall behind

00:37:33.650 --> 00:37:35.690
if you don't leave
right now, it's

00:37:35.690 --> 00:37:39.530
a peer-to-peer infrastructure.

00:37:39.530 --> 00:37:42.020
That means every
compute instance

00:37:42.020 --> 00:37:45.620
is running their own
transformation amongst itself.

00:37:45.620 --> 00:37:47.600
I think that's pretty cool.

00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:49.520
I eliminated the whole problem.

00:37:49.520 --> 00:37:51.920
One of the biggest
problems was the device

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:55.490
had to sit there and manage
all these migrations.

00:37:55.490 --> 00:37:57.260
Well, if you
eliminate that and you

00:37:57.260 --> 00:38:01.590
spread the compute through the
whole migration, guess what?

00:38:01.590 --> 00:38:03.530
There's no bottleneck
other than bandwidth.

00:38:03.530 --> 00:38:06.410
So once we procure
the bandwidth,

00:38:06.410 --> 00:38:10.769
that's when you hear terms
like, "unprecedented."

00:38:10.769 --> 00:38:12.810
That's when you hear words
like, "unprecedented,"

00:38:12.810 --> 00:38:13.893
or, I never heard of that.

00:38:13.893 --> 00:38:14.840
It's impossible.

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:17.190
Now, keep in mind
before we were acquired,

00:38:17.190 --> 00:38:19.184
we used to do this
magic all the time.

00:38:19.184 --> 00:38:20.600
And we would tell
people about it,

00:38:20.600 --> 00:38:22.100
and they would say you're
